Here's the Minizinc model to solve the coin change problem while minimizing the number of coins used:

```minizinc
% Coin values (in cents for clarity)
array[1..6] of int: coin_values = [1, 2, 5, 10, 25, 50];

% Available coins for each type
array[1..6] of int: available_coins = [20, 10, 15, 8, 4, 2];

% Change to give (199 euros = 19900 cents)
int: change = 19900;

% Decision variables: how many coins of each type to use
array[1..6] of var 0..max(available_coins): coin_counts;

% Constraint: total value must equal the change
constraint sum([coin_counts[i] * coin_values[i] | i in 1..6]) = change;

% Constraint: cannot use more coins than available
constraint forall(i in 1..6)(coin_counts[i] <= available_coins[i]);

% Objective: minimize total number of coins
var int: total_coins = sum(coin_counts);
solve minimize total_coins;

% Output the solution as JSON
output [
    "{",
    "\"" ++ "coin_counts" ++ "\": [" ++ join(", ", [show(coin_counts[i]) | i in 1..6]) ++ "]",
    "}"
];
```

This model:
1. Defines the coin values and available quantities
2. Sets up decision variables for how many coins of each type to use
3. Ensures the total value equals the required change (19900 cents)
4. Constrains the solution to use only available coins
5. Minimizes the total number of coins used
6. Outputs the solution in JSON format showing how many coins of each type to use

The solution will show the optimal way to make 199 euros (19900 cents) change using the fewest possible coins from the available quantities.

