Here's a MiniZinc model for the Social Golfers Problem with the given input parameters (4 weeks, 3 groups per week, and 3 golfers per group). The model ensures that no two golfers play together more than once:

```minizinc
include "globals.mzn";

int: n_weeks = 4;       % Number of weeks
int: n_groups = 3;      % Number of groups per week
int: group_size = 3;    % Number of golfers per group
int: n_golfers = n_groups * group_size; % Total number of golfers

set of int: Golfers = 1..n_golfers;
set of int: Weeks = 1..n_weeks;
set of int: Groups = 1..n_groups;

% Decision variable: assign[week, group, position] = golfer
array[Weeks, Groups, 1..group_size] of var Golfers: assign;

% Constraints

% 1. All golfers in each week are distinct (no golfer plays twice in same week)
constraint forall(w in Weeks) (
    all_different([assign[w,g,p] | g in Groups, p in 1..group_size])
);

% 2. Each group has exactly group_size distinct golfers
constraint forall(w in Weeks, g in Groups) (
    all_different([assign[w,g,p] | p in 1..group_size])
);

% 3. No two golfers play together more than once
constraint forall(g1, g2 in Golfers where g1 < g2) (
    sum([bool2int(
        exists(g in Groups, p1, p2 in 1..group_size where p1 != p2)(
            assign[w,g,p1] == g1 /\ assign[w,g,p2] == g2
        )
    ) | w in Weeks]) <= 1
);

% Symmetry breaking: order golfers within groups and order groups within weeks
constraint forall(w in Weeks, g in Groups, p in 1..group_size-1) (
    assign[w,g,p] < assign[w,g,p+1]
);

constraint forall(w in Weeks, g in Groups-1) (
    assign[w,g,1] < assign[w,g+1,1]
);

% First week is fixed to break symmetry
constraint forall(g in Groups, p in 1..group_size) (
    assign[1,g,p] = (g-1)*group_size + p
);

solve satisfy;

output [
    "{",
    "\"assign\": [",
    join(",\n", [
        "[" ++ join(", ", [
            "[" ++ join(", ", [show(assign[w,g,p]) | p in 1..group_size]) ++ "]"
            | g in Groups
        ]) ++ "]"
        | w in Weeks
    ]),
    "]",
    "}"
];
```

This model:
1. Defines the problem parameters (4 weeks, 3 groups per week, 3 golfers per group)
2. Creates a 3D array `assign` to represent which golfer is in which position of which group each week
3. Includes constraints to ensure:
   - No golfer plays twice in the same week
   - Groups have the correct size
   - No two golfers play together more than once
4. Adds symmetry breaking constraints to reduce the search space
5. Fixes the first week to break additional symmetry
6. Outputs the solution in JSON format showing the assignments for each week

The output will show the group assignments for each week, where each inner array represents a group containing the golfers assigned to it.

Note: For larger instances, this problem can become computationally intensive. The given parameters (4 weeks, 3 groups of 3) should be solvable relatively quickly.

